# (name, label, summary, caveat, [(title, url, publisher)])
C=[
("Sir Nicholas Winton","Organized the rescue of 669 children from Prague and told no one for about fifty years",
 "In 1939 Winton, a London stockbroker, organized eight trains that carried 669 children from Nazi-threatened Czechoslovakia to Britain and found foster homes for them. He told almost no one about it for about fifty years. The story became public in 1988, when his wife found documents about the children in their attic.",
 "Sources differ on what his wife found: a scrapbook in most accounts, a briefcase of lists and letters in the Rotary account. He became very well known after 1988, so the unseen part describes the decades before.",
 [("British Schindler, Holocaust hero dies at 106","https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Europe/2015/0701/British-Schindler-Holocaust-hero-dies-at-106","The Christian Science Monitor"),
  ("Nicholas Winton and the rescue of children from Czechoslovakia, 1938-1939","https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/nicholas-winton-and-the-rescue-of-children-from-czechoslovakia-1938-1939","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum"),
  ("Rotarian Sir Nicholas Winton dies peacefully aged 106","https://www.rotarygbi.org/news-archive/british-hero-rotarian-sir-nicholas-winton-dies-peacefully-aged-106","Rotary in Great Britain and Ireland")]),
("Irena Sendler","Smuggled children out of the Warsaw Ghetto and buried their real names in jars",
 "Sendler, a Polish social worker, helped get about 2,500 Jewish children out of the Warsaw Ghetto and placed them with families and institutions. She recorded their real names in code and buried the lists in jars under a neighbor's apple tree. The Gestapo arrested and tortured her in 1943, and she did not reveal the children's hiding places.",
 "The figure of 2,500 is a commonly cited estimate. Her story reached a wide audience late, after Kansas students researched it for a 2000 play. She said she did not consider herself a hero.",
 [("Irena Sendler","https://www.jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/biography/irenasendler.html","Jewish Virtual Library"),
  ("Irena Sendler","https://www.raoulwallenberg.net/saviors/polish/irena-sendler-409/","Raoul Wallenberg Foundation")]),
("Chiune Sugihara","Issued thousands of transit visas in Lithuania in 1940 and rarely spoke of it",
 "In summer 1940 Sugihara, Japan's consul in Kaunas, Lithuania, issued transit visas to Jewish refugees after Tokyo refused exceptions. The visas let thousands travel across the Soviet Union to Japan. His son said he rarely talked about it, and his story became known after an Israeli diplomat found him in 1968.",
 "Counts differ: 2,139 names on his list (Sugihara House), 2,178 (USHMM), at least 2,000 (JTA), and 6,000 or more people helped once families are counted. Sources also differ on whether he defied explicit orders.",
 [("Chiune Sugihara","https://sugiharahouse.com/en/chiune-sugihara","Sugihara House, Kaunas"),
  ("Museum statement on remembering humanitarian Chiune Sugihara","https://www.ushmm.org/information/press/press-releases/museum-statement-on-remembering-humanitarian-chiune-sugihara","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum"),
  ("Japanese prime minister visits Lithuanian museum honoring Holocaust rescuer Chiune Sugihara","https://www.jta.org/2018/01/16/global/japanese-prime-minister-visits-lithuanian-museum-honoring-holocaust-rescuer-chiune-sugihara","Jewish Telegraphic Agency")]),
("Jan Zwartendijk","A Dutch businessman whose \"Curacao visas\" opened an escape route, remembered by many only as \"Mr. Philips Radio\"",
 "In 1940 Zwartendijk, a Dutch Philips manager acting as honorary consul in Kaunas, issued papers saying no visa was needed to enter Curacao and other Dutch territories. These let Jewish refugees apply for Japanese transit visas. For years his name was unknown to many of the people he helped.",
 "Counts differ: more than 2,000 visas (LRT) and at least 2,345 recovered inscriptions. He was recognized by Yad Vashem in 1997 and by the Dutch government in 2023, after his death in 1976.",
 [("Jan Zwartendijk","https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/jan-zwartendijk","United States Holocaust Memorial Museum"),
  ("Former Dutch honorary consul in Kaunas receives posthumous award for saving Jews","https://www.lrt.lt/en/news-in-english/19/2077071/former-dutch-honorary-consul-in-kaunas-receives-posthumous-award-for-saving-jews","LRT, Lithuanian public broadcaster")]),
("Chuck Feeney","Gave away about eight billion dollars in secret",
 "Chuck Feeney, co-founder of Duty Free Shoppers, moved his stake in the business to a foundation that became The Atlantic Philanthropies. It gave to universities, hospitals and other causes without his name on the gifts, and many recipients did not know where the money came from. The foundation spent down its funds by 2016.",
 "His role became public in 1997, so the secrecy lasted about fifteen years. Totals vary: LA Times says about $8 billion, Cornell about $7 billion.",
 [("Elusive billionaire gives away his good fortune (Los Angeles Times, 2008)","https://www.atlanticphilanthropies.org/news/elusive-billionaire-gives-away-his-good-fortune","Los Angeles Times, via The Atlantic Philanthropies"),
  ("Chuck Feeney, Cornell's third founder, dies at 92","https://www.tech.cornell.edu/news/chuck-feeney-cornells-third-founder-dies-at-92/","Cornell Tech"),
  ("Charles Feeney, who made a fortune and then gave it away, dies at 92","https://www.bostonglobe.com/2023/10/09/metro/charles-feeney-who-made-fortune-then-gave-it-away-dies-92","The Boston Globe")]),
("The Kalamazoo Promise","Anonymous donors pay college tuition for a whole school district",
 "In November 2005 the Kalamazoo Public Schools announced a scholarship program funded by anonymous donors. It pays tuition and fees at public Michigan colleges and universities for district graduates, from 65 percent to 100 percent depending on how long the student attended. The donors have stayed unidentified.",
 "The Upjohn Institute reports over $180 million awarded to close to 7,000 graduates as of 2022. That total comes from one source. Some outlets have guessed at the donors' identities; none of these sources confirm it.",
 [("About the Kalamazoo Promise","https://www.upjohn.org/about-kalamazoo-promise","W.E. Upjohn Institute for Employment Research"),
  ("The Kalamazoo Promise Scholarship","https://www.educationnext.org/the-kalamazoo-promise-scholarship/","Education Next, Hoover Institution")]),
("Larry Stewart, the original Secret Santa","Handed strangers $100 bills for about 26 years before revealing his name",
 "Beginning in 1979, Larry Stewart of the Kansas City area gave cash to strangers each Christmas season, later in $100 bills, and kept his identity secret for about a quarter century. He gave away more than a million dollars. He revealed his name in late 2006 while being treated for cancer, to encourage others to be kind, and died in early 2007.",
 "Totals differ: more than $1 million (KCUR, NBC) and about $1.3 million (others). He became public by choice, so he was anonymous for most of the giving, not all of it.",
 [("Secret Santa no longer secret","https://spokesman.com/stories/2006/nov/19/secret-santa-no-longer-secret","The Spokesman-Review"),
  ("Friend continues late hero's Secret Santa","https://www.npr.org/2007/12/24/17571348/friend-continues-late-heros-secret-santa","NPR"),
  ("Secret Santa dies at age 58","https://www.kcur.org/2007-01-13/secret-santa-dies-at-age-58","KCUR, Kansas City public radio"),
  ("Secret Santa reveals himself in Kansas City","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna16327424","NBC News")]),
("Anonymous Kmart layaway donors, 2011","Strangers quietly paid off other families' toy layaways",
 "In December 2011, unnamed people at Kmart stores in Indianapolis, Omaha, Missoula and elsewhere paid off layaway balances for strangers, mostly toys and children's clothes. In Indianapolis a widow paid for dozens of people and asked them only to remember her husband, without giving her name.",
 "These were several separate donors, not one person, and they were not identified in the reports. Amounts differ by outlet (balances up to $800 in ABC's account; \"up to 50 people\" in others).",
 [("Kmart layaway shoppers touched by cash-carrying angel","https://abcnews.com/blogs/business/2011/12/kmart-layaway-shoppers-touched-by-cash-carrying-angel","ABC News"),
  ("Anonymous Kmart layaway donors popping up around the country","https://assets.christianpost.com/news/anonymous-kmart-layaway-donors-popping-up-around-the-country.html","The Christian Post")]),
("Ronald Read","A gas station attendant and janitor quietly left six million dollars to a hospital and a library",
 "Ronald Read worked at a gas station and later as a janitor in Brattleboro, Vermont, and was known as private and frugal. After he died in 2014, his estate gave $4.8 million to Brattleboro Memorial Hospital and $1.2 million to the Brooks Memorial Library. Neighbors did not know he had built a large fortune by picking stocks.",
 "This was a bequest revealed after his death, not giving he did while alive. His attorney put the estate at about $8 million.",
 [("Secret millionaire: Vermont janitor bequeaths fortune to hospital, library","https://www.today.com/today/amp/tdna1201","TODAY, NBC News"),
  ("How one retired janitor became a multi-millionaire","https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/USA-Update/2015/0206/How-one-retired-janitor-became-a-multi-millionaire","The Christian Science Monitor")]),
("A stranger's kidney starts a chain of 60 operations","One donor with no one to give to set off 30 transplants across 17 hospitals",
 "In 2011 Rick Ruzzamenti donated a kidney to a stranger, starting a chain run by the nonprofit National Kidney Registry. It grew to 30 donors and 30 recipients, 60 operations at 17 hospitals in 11 states over about four months. Many of the donors gave to a stranger because they could not give to a loved one, who then received a match through the exchange.",
 "Both sources describe the same chain, one from the organizer and one a news report. Most participants were not publicly named.",
 [("60 lives, 30 kidneys, all linked","https://www.kidneyregistry.com/news/60-lives-30-kidneys-all-linked","National Kidney Registry"),
  ("60 lives, 30 kidneys, all linked (New York Times reporting)","https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna46448895","NBC News")]),
("Why people give a kidney to a stranger","Interview studies of people who donate a kidney to someone they will never know",
 "A 2013 interview study of six prospective altruistic kidney donors found they described the urge to donate as compelling and mostly rejected personal gain as a motive. A 2020 abstract from UCLA interviewed 31 non-directed donors and grouped their reasons into inspiration from media, recognition of the broad need, and personal experience of loss.",
 "Both are small studies using different methods. The 2020 source is a conference abstract, not a journal article.",
 [("Challenor and Watts (2013), Health, City University of London repository","https://openaccess.city.ac.uk/id/eprint/4970/","City, University of London"),
  ("Why donate to a stranger? Motivations of 31 non-directed altruistic living kidney donors","https://atcmeetingabstracts.com/abstract/why-donate-to-a-stranger-exploring-the-major-motivations-of-31-non-directed-altruistic-living-kidney-donors/","American Transplant Congress")]),
("Anonymous marrow and stem cell donors","Registry donors give to patients they are not allowed to identify for at least a year",
 "Marrow and stem cell donors through registries such as NMDP and DKMS give to a patient they do not know. In the United States the two must stay anonymous for at least a year, with any letters passing through the transplant center. After that they can meet only if both agree in writing.",
 "Rules vary by country and transplant center, and some countries allow no contact at all. This documents the policy, not the number of donors.",
 [("Meeting your recipient","https://www.nmdp.org/transplant-basics/after-you-donate/contacting-your-recipient/","NMDP"),
  ("How is my patient?","https://www.dkms.org/donor-info/how-is-my-patient","DKMS"),
  ("The chance of a lifetime: how recipients meet their donors","https://www.giftoflife.org/posts/post/the-chance-of-a-lifetime-how-recipients-meet-their-donors","Gift of Life Marrow Registry")]),
("Lost wallets in 40 countries","Strangers returned lost wallets more often when more money was inside",
 "In a 2019 field experiment published in Science, researchers left more than 17,000 wallets with staff at banks, museums and other places in 355 cities across 40 countries. People were more likely to try to return a wallet when it held more money, and that held in nearly every country.",
 "The outcome was whether the recipient contacted the supposed owner, not a confirmed return. The $94 condition was run in only three countries. The study did not test refusing a reward.",
 [("Civic honesty around the globe (Science, 2019)","https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aau8712","Science"),
  ("Study: people more likely to return lost wallet with more money","https://www.sci.news/othersciences/psychology/civic-honesty-07337.html","Sci.News"),
  ("Scientists dropped 17,000 wallets to understand how honest we really are","https://www.sciencealert.com/scientists-dropped-17-000-wallets-to-understand-how-honest-we-really-are","ScienceAlert")]),
]
